1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,741
Imagine it's a Friday afternoon in the

2
00:00:02,741 --> 00:00:05,050
spring of nineteen seventy-five.

3
00:00:05,230 --> 00:00:07,354
You're sixty-five years old.

4
00:00:07,534 --> 00:00:10,170
After forty years at the same plant,

5
00:00:10,170 --> 00:00:11,561
or the same office,

6
00:00:11,561 --> 00:00:13,099
or the same railroad,

7
00:00:13,099 --> 00:00:16,247
your coworkers have gathered around a sheet

8
00:00:16,247 --> 00:00:16,613
cake.

9
00:00:16,793 --> 00:00:18,930
Somebody gives a short speech.

10
00:00:19,110 --> 00:00:21,374
Your boss hands you a gold watch.

11
00:00:21,554 --> 00:00:22,799
And on Monday morning,

12
00:00:22,799 --> 00:00:24,667
for the first time since you were

13
00:00:24,667 --> 00:00:26,478
a teenager, you don't have to go

14
00:00:26,478 --> 00:00:26,987
anywhere.

15
00:00:27,338 --> 00:00:30,099
If you retired in nineteen seventy-five,

16
00:00:30,099 --> 00:00:32,446
you were born around nineteen ten.

17
00:00:32,626 --> 00:00:35,461
You remembered the First World War as

18
00:00:35,461 --> 00:00:36,074
a child.

19
00:00:36,254 --> 00:00:38,310
You came of age during the Great

20
00:00:38,310 --> 00:00:39,017
Depression.

21
00:00:39,197 --> 00:00:41,666
You probably served in the Second World

22
00:00:41,666 --> 00:00:43,692
War, or worked in a factory that

23
00:00:43,692 --> 00:00:44,515
built for it.

24
00:00:44,695 --> 00:00:46,608
And now, after all of that,

25
00:00:46,608 --> 00:00:48,805
you were finally going to rest.

26
00:00:49,155 --> 00:00:51,598
Today, let's look at what that retirement

27
00:00:51,598 --> 00:00:52,730
really looked like.

28
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:54,562
Where the money came from,

29
00:00:54,562 --> 00:00:55,770
where people lived,

30
00:00:55,770 --> 00:00:57,040
what a day was like,

31
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:59,773
and what the world of nineteen seventy-five

32
00:00:59,773 --> 00:01:02,187
offered a person who had just finished

33
00:01:02,187 --> 00:01:03,394
a lifetime of work.

34
00:01:03,574 --> 00:01:05,803
Some of it will sound wonderful.

35
00:01:05,983 --> 00:01:08,096
Some of it will sound very hard.

36
00:01:08,447 --> 00:01:10,145
In nineteen seventy-five,

37
00:01:10,145 --> 00:01:12,591
the gold watch was a real tradition.

38
00:01:12,771 --> 00:01:16,099
Many companies gave one after twenty-five or

39
00:01:16,099 --> 00:01:17,915
thirty years of service.

40
00:01:18,095 --> 00:01:20,003
It was a way of saying thank

41
00:01:20,003 --> 00:01:21,502
you, and for many men,

42
00:01:21,502 --> 00:01:23,887
it was the most valuable thing they

43
00:01:23,887 --> 00:01:24,910
had ever owned.

44
00:01:25,090 --> 00:01:27,780
Plenty of those watches are still sitting

45
00:01:27,780 --> 00:01:29,420
in dresser drawers today.

46
00:01:29,770 --> 00:01:32,003
More important than the watch was the

47
00:01:32,003 --> 00:01:32,486
pension.

48
00:01:32,666 --> 00:01:34,110
In the mid-seventies,

49
00:01:34,110 --> 00:01:37,341
roughly four in ten private-sector workers were

50
00:01:37,341 --> 00:01:39,679
covered by a company pension plan.

51
00:01:39,859 --> 00:01:41,745
If you had worked for a big

52
00:01:41,745 --> 00:01:43,701
employer, like a steel mill,

53
00:01:43,701 --> 00:01:44,679
an auto plant,

54
00:01:44,679 --> 00:01:47,124
a utility, the phone company or the

55
00:01:47,124 --> 00:01:50,198
government, a check would arrive every month

56
00:01:50,198 --> 00:01:52,014
for the rest of your life.

57
00:01:52,365 --> 00:01:54,512
But pensions weren't always safe.

58
00:01:54,692 --> 00:01:57,442
When the Studebaker car plant in South

59
00:01:57,442 --> 00:02:00,698
Bend, Indiana closed in nineteen sixty-three,

60
00:02:00,698 --> 00:02:03,374
thousands of workers lost most of the

61
00:02:03,374 --> 00:02:05,617
pension they had been promised.

62
00:02:05,797 --> 00:02:08,785
Stories like that led Congress to pass

63
00:02:08,785 --> 00:02:12,244
a law called ERISA in nineteen seventy-four,

64
00:02:12,244 --> 00:02:15,782
which set rules to protect workers' pensions.

65
00:02:15,962 --> 00:02:18,588
For people retiring in seventy-five,

66
00:02:18,588 --> 00:02:19,828
it was brand new.

67
00:02:20,178 --> 00:02:21,448
For most retirees,

68
00:02:21,448 --> 00:02:24,624
the most important check was Social Security.

69
00:02:24,804 --> 00:02:26,347
And in the seventies,

70
00:02:26,347 --> 00:02:28,403
Social Security was growing.

71
00:02:28,583 --> 00:02:31,692
Congress raised benefits sharply in the early

72
00:02:31,692 --> 00:02:33,005
part of the decade,

73
00:02:33,005 --> 00:02:35,631
and starting in nineteen seventy-five,

74
00:02:35,631 --> 00:02:38,671
benefits rose automatically with the cost of

75
00:02:38,671 --> 00:02:39,915
living every year.

76
00:02:40,265 --> 00:02:41,923
That mattered enormously,

77
00:02:41,923 --> 00:02:44,443
because the seventies were a decade of

78
00:02:44,443 --> 00:02:45,106
inflation.

79
00:02:45,286 --> 00:02:48,327
In nineteen seventy-four and seventy-five,

80
00:02:48,327 --> 00:02:51,223
prices were rising by around ten percent

81
00:02:51,223 --> 00:02:51,729
a year.

82
00:02:51,909 --> 00:02:54,470
A retiree on a fixed income could

83
00:02:54,470 --> 00:02:56,487
watch the price of coffee,

84
00:02:56,487 --> 00:02:59,746
meat and gasoline climb month after month.

85
00:02:59,926 --> 00:03:02,986
The yearly increase in Social Security kept

86
00:03:02,986 --> 00:03:04,338
many people afloat.

87
00:03:04,688 --> 00:03:06,127
The results were dramatic.

88
00:03:06,307 --> 00:03:08,252
In nineteen fifty-nine,

89
00:03:08,252 --> 00:03:11,381
more than one in three Americans over

90
00:03:11,381 --> 00:03:13,749
sixty-five lived in poverty.

91
00:03:13,929 --> 00:03:15,350
By the mid-seventies,

92
00:03:15,350 --> 00:03:18,125
that number had dropped to around fifteen

93
00:03:18,125 --> 00:03:18,666
percent.

94
00:03:18,846 --> 00:03:21,761
For the first time in American history,

95
00:03:21,761 --> 00:03:23,854
growing old no longer meant,

96
00:03:23,854 --> 00:03:25,049
for most people,

97
00:03:25,049 --> 00:03:26,021
growing poor.

98
00:03:26,371 --> 00:03:28,425
The other great change was Medicare.

99
00:03:28,605 --> 00:03:30,975
It had only been signed into law

100
00:03:30,975 --> 00:03:33,270
in July of nineteen sixty-five,

101
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:35,343
by President Lyndon Johnson,

102
00:03:35,343 --> 00:03:38,156
at the Truman Library in Independence,

103
00:03:38,156 --> 00:03:38,822
Missouri.

104
00:03:39,002 --> 00:03:42,050
Harry Truman himself received the very first

105
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:43,019
Medicare card.

106
00:03:43,199 --> 00:03:45,841
So if you retired in seventy-five,

107
00:03:45,841 --> 00:03:48,482
Medicare was barely ten years old.

108
00:03:48,832 --> 00:03:51,994
Before Medicare, about half of older Americans

109
00:03:51,994 --> 00:03:54,126
had no health insurance at all.

110
00:03:54,306 --> 00:03:56,873
A single stay in the hospital could

111
00:03:56,873 --> 00:03:59,147
wipe out a lifetime of savings.

112
00:03:59,327 --> 00:04:01,485
Many older people simply didn't go to

113
00:04:01,485 --> 00:04:04,052
the doctor, because they couldn't afford it.

114
00:04:04,232 --> 00:04:07,282
By the time you retired in seventy-five,

115
00:04:07,282 --> 00:04:11,094
almost every American over sixty-five was covered.

116
00:04:11,444 --> 00:04:13,882
Medicine itself was still simpler.

117
00:04:14,062 --> 00:04:15,859
There were fewer drugs,

118
00:04:15,859 --> 00:04:18,439
fewer scans and fewer operations.

119
00:04:18,619 --> 00:04:21,835
Heart bypass surgery was still fairly new.

120
00:04:22,015 --> 00:04:25,200
Many doctors still made house calls in

121
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:26,206
small towns.

122
00:04:26,386 --> 00:04:28,761
And the family doctor who had delivered

123
00:04:28,761 --> 00:04:30,953
your children might well be the same

124
00:04:30,953 --> 00:04:33,329
one who looked after you in retirement.

125
00:04:33,679 --> 00:04:37,152
Most retirees in nineteen seventy-five stayed right

126
00:04:37,152 --> 00:04:38,241
where they were.

127
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:40,428
They lived in the house they had

128
00:04:40,428 --> 00:04:42,498
bought in the forties or fifties,

129
00:04:42,498 --> 00:04:43,188
and by now,

130
00:04:43,188 --> 00:04:44,818
the mortgage was paid off.

131
00:04:44,998 --> 00:04:47,065
No rent, no house payment.

132
00:04:47,245 --> 00:04:50,173
Just taxes, the electric bill and the

133
00:04:50,173 --> 00:04:51,123
heating oil.

134
00:04:51,303 --> 00:04:52,442
For many families,

135
00:04:52,442 --> 00:04:54,719
the house was the biggest thing they

136
00:04:54,719 --> 00:04:55,099
owned.

137
00:04:55,450 --> 00:04:57,330
But a growing number headed south.

138
00:04:57,510 --> 00:05:00,821
Florida and Arizona became the great retirement

139
00:05:00,821 --> 00:05:02,723
destinations of the decade.

140
00:05:02,903 --> 00:05:04,683
Places like Sun City,

141
00:05:04,683 --> 00:05:08,072
Arizona, which opened in nineteen sixty,

142
00:05:08,072 --> 00:05:09,851
offered golf courses,

143
00:05:09,851 --> 00:05:14,851
swimming pools, shuffleboard courts and recreation centers,

144
00:05:14,851 --> 00:05:18,240
all designed for people over fifty-five.

145
00:05:18,590 --> 00:05:21,374
In Florida, retirees from New York,

146
00:05:21,374 --> 00:05:25,271
New Jersey, Ohio and Michigan filled condominiums

147
00:05:25,271 --> 00:05:28,294
and mobile home parks from Miami Beach

148
00:05:28,294 --> 00:05:28,772
to St.

149
00:05:28,952 --> 00:05:29,799
Petersburg.

150
00:05:29,979 --> 00:05:32,469
Many of them had spent their whole

151
00:05:32,469 --> 00:05:34,008
lives shoveling snow.

152
00:05:34,188 --> 00:05:36,516
Now they spent their winters in short

153
00:05:36,516 --> 00:05:38,908
sleeves, playing cards by the pool and

154
00:05:38,908 --> 00:05:41,363
complaining happily about the humidity.

155
00:05:41,713 --> 00:05:44,376
And many retirees lived close to their

156
00:05:44,376 --> 00:05:45,428
grown children.

157
00:05:45,608 --> 00:05:47,752
It was common for a grandmother to

158
00:05:47,752 --> 00:05:49,076
live down the street,

159
00:05:49,076 --> 00:05:51,157
or in a little apartment over the

160
00:05:51,157 --> 00:05:51,599
garage.

161
00:05:51,779 --> 00:05:55,594
Grandparents babysat, cooked Sunday dinners and helped

162
00:05:55,594 --> 00:05:57,572
out in a hundred small ways.

163
00:05:57,752 --> 00:06:00,350
Families were spread out less than they

164
00:06:00,350 --> 00:06:02,082
would be in later decades.

165
00:06:02,433 --> 00:06:04,741
So what did a typical day look

166
00:06:04,741 --> 00:06:05,126
like?

167
00:06:05,306 --> 00:06:06,908
For many retired men,

168
00:06:06,908 --> 00:06:08,204
it started early,

169
00:06:08,204 --> 00:06:09,195
out of habit.

170
00:06:09,375 --> 00:06:11,870
Forty years of punching a clock doesn't

171
00:06:11,870 --> 00:06:13,021
go away overnight.

172
00:06:13,201 --> 00:06:15,302
Coffee at the kitchen table.

173
00:06:15,482 --> 00:06:16,956
The morning paper,

174
00:06:16,956 --> 00:06:18,594
read cover to cover.

175
00:06:18,774 --> 00:06:21,045
Maybe a walk down to the corner

176
00:06:21,045 --> 00:06:23,682
coffee shop, where the same group of

177
00:06:23,682 --> 00:06:25,879
retired men met every morning.

178
00:06:26,229 --> 00:06:27,622
Then there were projects.

179
00:06:27,802 --> 00:06:29,508
Fixing the screen door.

180
00:06:29,688 --> 00:06:30,989
Painting the garage.

181
00:06:31,169 --> 00:06:33,264
Working in the vegetable garden,

182
00:06:33,264 --> 00:06:35,686
which for many retirees was the pride

183
00:06:35,686 --> 00:06:36,602
of the summer.

184
00:06:36,782 --> 00:06:40,575
Tomatoes, beans, zucchini and more cucumbers than

185
00:06:40,575 --> 00:06:42,587
any family could ever eat.

186
00:06:42,767 --> 00:06:44,880
The extra went to the neighbors,

187
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:46,993
whether they wanted them or not.

188
00:06:47,344 --> 00:06:48,303
For many women,

189
00:06:48,303 --> 00:06:50,095
retirement looked different,

190
00:06:50,095 --> 00:06:52,719
because their work at home never stopped.

191
00:06:52,899 --> 00:06:54,080
They still cooked,

192
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:56,637
cleaned and kept the household running.

193
00:06:56,817 --> 00:06:58,818
But now there was more time for

194
00:06:58,818 --> 00:07:00,626
church groups, bridge clubs,

195
00:07:00,626 --> 00:07:03,853
quilting circles and volunteering at the hospital.

196
00:07:04,033 --> 00:07:06,355
Some said that having their husband home

197
00:07:06,355 --> 00:07:08,503
all day was the hardest adjustment of

198
00:07:08,503 --> 00:07:08,735
all.

199
00:07:09,085 --> 00:07:12,069
Afternoons might mean a game of checkers

200
00:07:12,069 --> 00:07:13,635
at the senior center,

201
00:07:13,635 --> 00:07:15,500
a nap, or a soap opera on

202
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:16,619
the television.

203
00:07:16,799 --> 00:07:20,651
In seventy-five, that television probably had about

204
00:07:20,651 --> 00:07:22,539
a dozen channels at most,

205
00:07:22,539 --> 00:07:24,955
and many towns had only three or

206
00:07:24,955 --> 00:07:25,333
four.

207
00:07:25,513 --> 00:07:28,035
Evenings meant dinner at five-thirty,

208
00:07:28,035 --> 00:07:30,080
the news with Walter Cronkite,

209
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:32,466
and maybe The Lawrence Welk Show on

210
00:07:32,466 --> 00:07:33,489
Saturday night.

211
00:07:33,839 --> 00:07:37,736
Getting around in nineteen seventy-five usually meant

212
00:07:37,736 --> 00:07:39,133
a big American car.

213
00:07:39,313 --> 00:07:41,303
A Buick, an Oldsmobile,

214
00:07:41,303 --> 00:07:44,159
a Chrysler or a Cadillac if you'd

215
00:07:44,159 --> 00:07:45,025
done well.

216
00:07:45,205 --> 00:07:47,225
Long, heavy and comfortable,

217
00:07:47,225 --> 00:07:49,100
with a soft ride and a big

218
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:49,605
engine.

219
00:07:49,785 --> 00:07:52,242
Many retirees bought one last new car

220
00:07:52,242 --> 00:07:54,034
right after the gold watch,

221
00:07:54,034 --> 00:07:55,827
and kept it for the rest of

222
00:07:55,827 --> 00:07:56,623
their lives.

223
00:07:56,974 --> 00:07:59,226
But gasoline had become a worry.

224
00:07:59,406 --> 00:08:02,856
The oil embargo of nineteen seventy-three had

225
00:08:02,856 --> 00:08:05,463
caused long lines at gas stations,

226
00:08:05,463 --> 00:08:07,150
and prices had jumped.

227
00:08:07,330 --> 00:08:09,190
By nineteen seventy-five,

228
00:08:09,190 --> 00:08:12,017
a gallon of gas cost about fifty-seven

229
00:08:12,017 --> 00:08:14,770
cents, almost double what it had been

230
00:08:14,770 --> 00:08:16,258
a few years earlier.

231
00:08:16,438 --> 00:08:19,186
Some retirees parked the big car and

232
00:08:19,186 --> 00:08:20,943
started taking the bus.

233
00:08:21,293 --> 00:08:23,707
And retirees discovered travel.

234
00:08:23,887 --> 00:08:26,918
Motor coach tours took groups of seniors

235
00:08:26,918 --> 00:08:29,116
to see the fall colors in New

236
00:08:29,116 --> 00:08:32,071
England, the Grand Canyon or the cherry

237
00:08:32,071 --> 00:08:33,814
blossoms in Washington.

238
00:08:33,994 --> 00:08:36,543
Some bought a travel trailer or a

239
00:08:36,543 --> 00:08:39,169
motor home and spent months on the

240
00:08:39,169 --> 00:08:39,555
road.

241
00:08:39,735 --> 00:08:42,259
For people who had rarely traveled during

242
00:08:42,259 --> 00:08:43,490
their working years,

243
00:08:43,490 --> 00:08:45,029
it was a whole new world.

244
00:08:45,380 --> 00:08:46,935
What did it cost to live?

245
00:08:47,115 --> 00:08:49,076
In nineteen seventy-five,

246
00:08:49,076 --> 00:08:51,900
a loaf of bread was about thirty-six

247
00:08:51,900 --> 00:08:55,509
cents, a dozen eggs about seventy-seven cents,

248
00:08:55,509 --> 00:08:58,882
and a pound of hamburger around eighty-five

249
00:08:58,882 --> 00:08:59,352
cents.

250
00:08:59,532 --> 00:09:02,261
A first-class stamp was ten cents,

251
00:09:02,261 --> 00:09:04,990
and then thirteen cents by the end

252
00:09:04,990 --> 00:09:05,953
of the year.

253
00:09:06,133 --> 00:09:09,012
A movie ticket cost around two dollars.

254
00:09:09,362 --> 00:09:11,386
Those numbers sound tiny today,

255
00:09:11,386 --> 00:09:14,064
but the average Social Security check for

256
00:09:14,064 --> 00:09:16,351
a retired worker was only about two

257
00:09:16,351 --> 00:09:17,918
hundred dollars a month.

258
00:09:18,098 --> 00:09:20,909
A couple living on Social Security alone

259
00:09:20,909 --> 00:09:23,439
had to be careful with every dollar.

260
00:09:23,619 --> 00:09:27,039
Clipping coupons, buying day-old bread and saving

261
00:09:27,039 --> 00:09:28,994
string were not just habits.

262
00:09:29,174 --> 00:09:30,289
They were survival.

263
00:09:30,639 --> 00:09:32,958
The lucky ones had three legs to

264
00:09:32,958 --> 00:09:33,611
stand on.

265
00:09:33,791 --> 00:09:37,323
Social Security, a company pension and some

266
00:09:37,323 --> 00:09:38,966
savings in the bank,

267
00:09:38,966 --> 00:09:42,088
often in a passbook savings account or

268
00:09:42,088 --> 00:09:44,634
in United States savings bonds.

269
00:09:44,814 --> 00:09:47,148
People called it the three-legged stool.

270
00:09:47,328 --> 00:09:48,248
With all three,

271
00:09:48,248 --> 00:09:50,579
retirement could be truly comfortable.

272
00:09:50,759 --> 00:09:51,686
With only one,

273
00:09:51,686 --> 00:09:53,209
it could be very tight.

274
00:09:53,559 --> 00:09:57,269
Retirement in nineteen seventy-five wasn't easy for

275
00:09:57,269 --> 00:09:57,924
everyone.

276
00:09:58,104 --> 00:10:01,300
Many people retired because their bodies simply

277
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:01,912
wore out.

278
00:10:02,092 --> 00:10:03,704
Decades in mines,

279
00:10:03,704 --> 00:10:06,929
mills and fields left men with bad

280
00:10:06,929 --> 00:10:09,964
backs, bad lungs and bad hearts.

281
00:10:10,144 --> 00:10:11,666
In the mid-seventies,

282
00:10:11,666 --> 00:10:14,639
a man who reached sixty-five could expect

283
00:10:14,639 --> 00:10:17,685
to live about another thirteen or fourteen

284
00:10:17,685 --> 00:10:18,120
years.

285
00:10:18,470 --> 00:10:20,617
Loneliness was real too.

286
00:10:20,797 --> 00:10:23,576
Many women outlived their husbands by ten

287
00:10:23,576 --> 00:10:24,524
years or more.

288
00:10:24,704 --> 00:10:27,642
Friends moved to Florida or passed away.

289
00:10:27,822 --> 00:10:30,492
Children moved to other states for work.

290
00:10:30,672 --> 00:10:33,118
Nursing homes were growing fast in the

291
00:10:33,118 --> 00:10:36,016
seventies, and some had serious problems with

292
00:10:36,016 --> 00:10:36,338
care.

293
00:10:36,518 --> 00:10:38,078
For many older people,

294
00:10:38,078 --> 00:10:40,064
the fear of ending up in one

295
00:10:40,064 --> 00:10:41,057
was very real.

296
00:10:41,407 --> 00:10:42,317
And for many,

297
00:10:42,317 --> 00:10:44,769
the hardest part was losing a sense

298
00:10:44,769 --> 00:10:45,540
of purpose.

299
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:47,645
A man who had been a foreman

300
00:10:47,645 --> 00:10:50,119
for twenty years was suddenly just a

301
00:10:50,119 --> 00:10:50,944
man at home.

302
00:10:51,124 --> 00:10:54,229
Some found new purpose in volunteering,

303
00:10:54,229 --> 00:10:56,140
church or grandchildren.

304
00:10:56,320 --> 00:10:57,423
Others struggled.

305
00:10:57,603 --> 00:11:00,311
Doctors and writers in the seventies started

306
00:11:00,311 --> 00:11:02,896
talking openly about the emotional side of

307
00:11:02,896 --> 00:11:04,743
retirement for the first time.

308
00:11:05,093 --> 00:11:07,112
And yet, for many people,

309
00:11:07,112 --> 00:11:10,989
retirement in nineteen seventy-five was the best

310
00:11:10,989 --> 00:11:12,604
time of their lives.

311
00:11:12,784 --> 00:11:15,243
They had survived the Depression and the

312
00:11:15,243 --> 00:11:15,489
war.

313
00:11:15,669 --> 00:11:18,045
They had raised families and paid off

314
00:11:18,045 --> 00:11:18,816
their homes.

315
00:11:18,996 --> 00:11:20,212
Now they had time,

316
00:11:20,212 --> 00:11:22,441
a steady check and a country that

317
00:11:22,441 --> 00:11:24,062
had, for the first time,

318
00:11:24,062 --> 00:11:26,292
made a real promise to look after

319
00:11:26,292 --> 00:11:27,575
its older citizens.

320
00:11:27,926 --> 00:11:30,020
They had grandchildren to spoil,

321
00:11:30,020 --> 00:11:32,245
and many of them did exactly that.

322
00:11:32,425 --> 00:11:35,838
Five-dollar bills tucked into birthday cards.

323
00:11:36,018 --> 00:11:38,084
Candy in a dish by the door.

324
00:11:38,264 --> 00:11:39,843
Fishing trips at the lake.

325
00:11:40,023 --> 00:11:42,461
Summer weeks at grandma's house.

326
00:11:42,641 --> 00:11:44,272
If you were a child in the

327
00:11:44,272 --> 00:11:47,345
seventies, you may remember a retired grandparent

328
00:11:47,345 --> 00:11:49,289
who seemed to have all the time

329
00:11:49,289 --> 00:11:50,606
in the world for you.

330
00:11:50,956 --> 00:11:53,371
They also had something we've lost a

331
00:11:53,371 --> 00:11:54,578
little: community.

332
00:11:54,758 --> 00:11:57,126
The same neighbors for thirty years.

333
00:11:57,306 --> 00:11:58,427
The same church,

334
00:11:58,427 --> 00:11:59,338
the same pew.

335
00:11:59,518 --> 00:12:00,724
The same barber,

336
00:12:00,724 --> 00:12:01,854
the same diner,

337
00:12:01,854 --> 00:12:03,059
the same doctor.

338
00:12:03,239 --> 00:12:04,829
When someone was sick,

339
00:12:04,829 --> 00:12:07,430
casseroles appeared on the doorstep.

340
00:12:07,610 --> 00:12:10,081
Retirement happened in a place where people

341
00:12:10,081 --> 00:12:10,942
knew your name.

342
00:12:11,293 --> 00:12:13,266
Compare that with retirement today.

343
00:12:13,446 --> 00:12:14,720
People live longer,

344
00:12:14,720 --> 00:12:17,336
often into their eighties and nineties.

345
00:12:17,516 --> 00:12:20,423
Most private pensions have been replaced by

346
00:12:20,423 --> 00:12:23,532
four-oh-one-k plans, which depend on the stock

347
00:12:23,532 --> 00:12:24,006
market.

348
00:12:24,186 --> 00:12:26,206
Health care costs far more.

349
00:12:26,386 --> 00:12:29,323
And many people keep working well past

350
00:12:29,323 --> 00:12:32,260
sixty-five, by choice or by necessity.

351
00:12:32,611 --> 00:12:33,557
In many ways,

352
00:12:33,557 --> 00:12:36,904
the retirees of nineteen seventy-five were the

353
00:12:36,904 --> 00:12:40,251
first generation to enjoy a modern retirement,

354
00:12:40,251 --> 00:12:41,778
with Social Security,

355
00:12:41,778 --> 00:12:44,325
Medicare and a pension all at once.

356
00:12:44,505 --> 00:12:47,050
Their parents had mostly worked until they

357
00:12:47,050 --> 00:12:50,020
couldn't anymore, then lived with their children.

358
00:12:50,200 --> 00:12:52,754
It was a remarkable change in just

359
00:12:52,754 --> 00:12:53,880
one generation.

360
00:12:54,230 --> 00:12:56,779
If you remember a parent or grandparent

361
00:12:56,779 --> 00:12:58,675
who retired in the seventies,

362
00:12:58,675 --> 00:13:00,244
you remember that world.

363
00:13:00,424 --> 00:13:02,537
The gold watch on the dresser.

364
00:13:02,717 --> 00:13:04,121
The garden out back.

365
00:13:04,301 --> 00:13:06,414
The big car in the driveway.

366
00:13:06,594 --> 00:13:09,162
The bowl of candy and the five-dollar

367
00:13:09,162 --> 00:13:09,509
bill.

368
00:13:09,689 --> 00:13:11,281
They earned every bit of it,

369
00:13:11,281 --> 00:13:13,045
and they spent it on the people

370
00:13:13,045 --> 00:13:13,671
they loved.

371
00:13:14,021 --> 00:13:15,890
So tell us in the comments.

372
00:13:16,070 --> 00:13:17,986
Who in your family retired in the

373
00:13:17,986 --> 00:13:20,366
seventies, and what do you remember about

374
00:13:20,366 --> 00:13:21,352
their retirement?

375
00:13:21,532 --> 00:13:23,138
And if you're retired today,

376
00:13:23,138 --> 00:13:25,259
how does your life compare to theirs?

377
00:13:25,439 --> 00:13:26,879
We read every comment.

378
00:13:27,059 --> 00:13:30,313
Next time, we're going trick-or-treating in nineteen

379
00:13:30,313 --> 00:13:31,064
fifty-eight.

380
00:13:31,244 --> 00:13:33,177
Thank you for spending this time with

381
00:13:33,177 --> 00:13:33,334
me.
